            <ArticleTitle>Bilateral chronic compartment syndrome of the thenar muscles: A case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>215-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.main.2015.06.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1297-3203(15)00094-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This case report features a patient with chronic bilateral compartment syndrome of the thenar muscles, which had appeared spontaneously 8 years earlier. The condition was progressive and symptoms recently had become worse. Pressure measurements were performed three times to establish the diagnosis: before, immediately after and 10 minutes after hand exertion. Pain relief was achieved by performing a fasciotomy of the right thenar compartment.</AbstractText>
